    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d522">

                <pb facs="0527" n="517" xml:id="pg517"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">832.796/299a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Caffery</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-04-07T20:00:00-05:00">April 7, 1941—8 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>178. Personal from the Under Secretary. Interested American oil companies are
                    being asked to withhold further supplies of aviation gasoline and lubricants
                    from Lati.<anchor n="52" xml:id="d522-f1"/> Please take this matter up
                    informally with Aranha and endeavor to ascertain whether the Brazilian
                    Government would receive favorably a request for its cooperation in this matter.
                    The oil companies have expressed the view that, in spite of their desire to
                    comply with the Department’s wishes, they might be confronted with contrary
                    instructions from the Brazilian Government. [Welles.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d522-f1" n="52" xml:id="d522-f1-content">
                <p>For pro-Axis activity of Lati, see Stetson Conn and Byron Fairchild, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">The Framework of Hemisphere Defense</hi>, in the series
                        <hi rendition="#italic">United States Army in World War II: The Western
                        Hemisphere</hi> (Washington, Government Printing Office, 1960), pp.
                    247–248.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
